 if somebody wanted to  know from us all the data that we held  on them if you remember it's very very  fragmented with lots of people lots of  data and it was very difficult for us to  pull all of that together and return  somebody a kind of readable file with  here's all the information we have on  you it was possible but it meant a lot  of phone calls and CSV files being  copied around and no we forgot to ask  Fred in you know mighty also we could  opt out of things like email marketing  and so on but we weren't easily able to  stop collecting an individual's data and  another requirement under GPR is that  you can collect the data but you can't  processes you're not allowed to use it  for you know targeted marketing and so  on and that's something we couldn't do  before either and finally you have the  right to have data that you can prove is  incorrect amended or to ask that your  data was completely deleted and both of  those we couldn't really do the Hadoop  based approach we had before the data  was pretty much right only from our  point of view so if we'd had to remove a  user's data we would have had to copy  all but that data assuming you just said  drop the old you've just come burst  and not really operationally feasible so  we had a bunch of challenges that we had  a you know legal obligation to comply  with that our old system couldn't meet  that actually was the thing that tipped  this over the edge so the data science  team had wanted something like this in  place for a long time to make their  lives easier gdpr was the thing that  finally gave us the business driver  we're gonna have to spend some money  we're gonna have to do a bunch of work  to to get to where we need to be why not  just build a system we know that we need  and we know that we want rather than  just try and patch out the old system in  some way if we had to spend the money  anyway why not spend it in the right  place rather than just spending it  patching up an old system so the new  system all the nature is in one place  implicitly we have pointers to where it  came from so if we do need to find  additional sources for deletion or  something we can remember all the data  ends up in SUV but that's not  necessarily the only place there are  operational databases that will have  other copies and also we're able to  increase the level of governance we've  got around our data so one of the things  that we have been able to do is to  separate data which contains PII and to  provide additional views of that data  with the PII masked now some people will  read need the real raw PIR you know  imagine customer service if someone  phones up and says hey look I've paid  for this item in-game and I didn't get  it my inventory customer service needs  to actually have the PII to speak to  that person and verify they are who they  are going to their game inventory figure  out what they've got and resolve the  problem but most people don't need  actual customer data they just need to  know for example that these players are  unique or that they can do a you know  join across tables and make sure that  you know this gameplay data is matched  with that identity data without knowing  who that person actually is so the way  we did that is I mentioned that we have  two game projects two projects within  bigquery for each game what we have is  one which contains the raw data which is  very locked down and two it's very few  people have access and then we have  another which has an identical set of  schema but the  any PII columns in the original data are  represented by hashed versions of that  PII so it's unique but it's not  personally identifiable unless you know  what that hash function is and the hash  function is rotated every week  automatically so from our point of view  even if somebody takes an extract and  has a snapshot of what that mapping is  in a few days it's gonna be obsolete  so we're limiting our risk there's no  zero risk you can't you know you have to  live with some level of risk assuming  you're not going to completely distrust  everybody in your company to work with  data but we're able to manage the risk  appropriately people that need the data  have access to it people that don't need  the data were able to kind of protect  them to be honest from having to worry  too much about it so on to the results  I'm going to talk briefly about four  experiments that we've run with the data  since we started the first is targeting  some Facebook Ads for shadow of the Tomb  Raider so what we did here and this is  some phenomenal work my team worked on  the actual segmentation in the look like  audience piece the analytics and  insights team built the actual segments  the marketing team at Idols Montreal did  a fantastic job in in the creative that  will have a look at in a minute and what  we did was we identified six segments in  our - this is rise of the Tomb Raider so  the previous version of the game  previous iteration we looked at the data  that we had for gameplay  of rise of the Tomb Raider we segmented  that user base using a number of  attributes look at them in a minute and  then we had a number of different  creatives we had six 30-second TV spots  that we used we combined that with a  bunch of different copy lines as well  for a different calls to action and we  multivariate says a/b tested he is  actually multivariate testing to find  out which creatives resonated best with  which segments of the audience  so these are roughly speaking the the  six kinds of players that we identified  we've given them all kind of nicknames  veteran action junkie solver dabble a  completionist and try list there are  obviously numeric values underneath this  that we're using to produce those  segmentations it's not a straight binary  slice in three dimensions we've got  actually a number of different  attributes here there's a kind of  cluster analysis process going on some  of these are obvious we've got things  like game play and you know as a  gameplay completion percentage  completion and things like that some of  those obviously you know if you look at  these these descriptions on screen you  can tell what some of those were there  are others they're a little bit more  sophisticated so for example action  junkie takes risk frequently attempting  challenging obstacles that's one of the  measures that contributes to that none  of these are simple they're all kind of  combined measures but one of the  measures that contributes to that is  what the UK legal system calls death by  misadventure so if if Lara doesn't make  a jump or falls off a cliff edge or  drowns or you know is killed other than  by an NPC then we regard that as you  know adrenaline-fueled  you know lust for challenge or something  so that's something the percentage death  by NPC versus kind of environmental  catastrophe  is something that code codes for action  junkie so you've got those different  segments there and what we've done is  the team put together six six six  30-second spots they're not a one-to-one  match for those six segments that  happened to be six in each that's kind  of coincidence these spots I've got I'm  going to show all of them but we've got  a ten-second clip for each of them at  the moment that I'll show you things to  watch out for when you look at there's a  little caption we can say it says TV  spot on the right here that tells you  kind of what the theme of that clip is  they've also got very different music  and different feel to them so if we run  through that and then I'll talk a little  bit about it in a second do you realize  the tragedy you have induced what have I  done  because I could not stop for death he  kindly stopped for me by taking the key  you said the apocalypse emotional  so you can see we've got a number of  different clips there they've all got a  very different look and feel some are  darker some one lighter some have got  you know more upbeat music than others  there are some which have dialogue and  focus on the character or a storyline  others which focus on stunts and things  like that and the idea was to look at  the the segment's we had of the  different types of data and basically to  match those clips and figure out which  resonated better and then also as I said  earlier the the copyright did copy lines  that we had on the ads as well and  it's the one thing we couldn't get  working thanks so to cut straight to the  results we found that the segmented ads  on bet on average the segmented  campaigns performed twice as well as the  unsegmented campaign so we had some  benchmark campaigns that we'd run before  where we did rest pretty much the same  audience with the same creative but with  the same creative across the whole  audience the click-through rate was on  average about literally almost exactly  double across all of these campaigns  combined some performed even better than  that some a little bit worse than than  double but the average was double so  we're really happy with that the other  way of looking at it of course is cost  per click so we've either doubled our  click-through rate or half dollar  marketing costs or the same number of  clicks so phenomenally successful from  our point of view again this is you know  early days but the early indications of  what we were able to do with the data  that we couldn't before have been really  useful remember before we had that  siloed data we weren't able for example  to take the siloed data and match that  to Facebook data to build Facebook  audiences which we could now um next  this may not look you may not be able to  see the detail on this but basically  this is the Kingdom Hearts website  Kingdom Hearts came out earlier this  year and we had the the website user  journey there which is fairly  traditional we've got a straight landing  page and then there's a you know user  journey you can imagine going through  the history the franchise details about  this particular game and then a Buy Now  page this was built by the team in LA  did a fantastic job on the website but  what we wanted to do was try and make  sure that we were getting maximum use  out of that and again looking at the  segmenting the the audience for that  we've got sources of data for people who  were completely new to the franchise  we're starting on the landing page and  stepping through that journey is the  right thing to do we have other people  who have got a high kind of purchase  intent already  dropping them straight into the by page  is the right thing to do  but equally if you get that the wrong  way around the last thing you want to do  is take somebody who's ready to buy and  bore them by taking them through a whole  history of the franchise they already  know or for somebody who doesn't really  know much that has just heard about this  Pixar thing that we've done and wants to  you know maybe they've picked up some  marketing on the Disney side of things  and doesn't know much about the game  dropping them straight and a by now  Paige feels really abrupt so what we did  was look at the sources of the traffic  analyze previous kind of right route  results from those sources and then have  the basically the consumer dropped into  that user journey from those ads what we  felt was the right place for them and  this performed even better than the Tomb  Raider campaign we got click-through  rates increased by 144 percent here  click throughs to to retailer links and  overall conversion increased by twenty  percent so again really happy with the  results from that site um a couple of  others I'll touch on very briefly before  we talk about next steps we with life is  strange too episodic game we localized  into a lot more languages than we did  for life is strange one life is strange  too we saw that we had users in new  markets like Russia and so on but the  game itself wasn't localized into those  languages so for two we localized into  those languages what we wanted to do is  measure the effectiveness the  effectiveness of that we can't  necessarily just look at sales data and  say we said I'll double the number of  units we need to look at other data as  well  user data where people are playing the  game correlating that with purchase  because obviously there will be you know  in different markets other than there  are sources of you know gameplay other  than people just buying their own copy  there's lots of second-hand there's  piracy and so on as well so  understanding that helped us really  figure out how we were going to optimize  our marketing our asourian localization  spends in future um so we'll be having  done this what we've found is that that  localization  effort having analyzed the data using  the data in bigquery we can find that  we've got in some countries double the  share of users for this version than we  had it previously so that share not  absolutely numbers so the indication  there is that localizing to those  languages has really had an impact on  increasing take-up in those countries  and finally timing analysis for episodic  games this is something that we're doing  at the moment that works ongoing but  what we're trying to do is look at  improving our sales forecasting and  eventually get to the point where we can  actually control our sales curve to some  extent by affecting the timing of  episodic releases for some game some  players will be super excited about a  game and pre-order at you know release  for other games they may play the first  episode and then buy a season pass to  enjoy the rest once they sort of bought  in they're treating the first episode is  a kind of you know trial um  player see they're maybe those people  are buying a collector's edition at the  end or perhaps they've you know maybe  they've played the whole game if they  haven't but they they're buying much  later in the sequence but then you've  got potentially player D which is a  group we didn't get at all who maybe  played the initial free first episode  and then just dropped out of the process  entirely so understanding what that  looks like how those those player bases  segment out and how the timing of those  releases affect that it's something  we're doing at the moment in order to  improve not only our forecasting in the  short term but long term the ability to  actually control that that sales  pipeline so some basic examples there I  think as we've said we've now got to a  situation where there's a lot more  experimentation going on with all those  constraints that we mentioned earlier  have gone away we've been really happy  with the results that we've got from the  platform we've got a really solid  relationship with the the GCP team where  we feel we're getting a lot of support  where we need it and you know fantastic  tech to build on we have worked on a  number of the challenges that we had for  example the the schema migration  and got great results there we have a  happy smiling DPO I would have shown you  a picture but that's PII and I'm not  allowed to do it without written consent  so but we're comfortable now that we're  a lot more secure than we were in our  kind of governance around the data and  this is the first real cloud native  application that we've built  we had infrastructure in public cloud  before we had web servers deployed on  kubernetes in different environments but  we hadn't really taken a leap and built  anything cloud native what we were doing  really is is infrastructure as a service  so it was effectively just not paying  for the hard way  capex but this is you know the first  time that we've really got into that and  it's been incredibly successful so  what's next for us in terms of the  single game of view platform well to  torture the Pokemon analogy we've got  some legendary data sources that we  still wanna catch there are some games  which aren't yet in this system for  various reasons and we're you know  having got these initial proof points  we're now able to kind of bring some of  those data sources in and open up those  conversations um a year in we've also  got some good use cases where we can  justify automating or building further  tooling around the platform so things  like we mentioned earlier the ability to  filter users and to either capture or  not capture data or to process or not  process data and having that process  automated and API driven is something  that we're working on at the moment  we're looking at a cost cross-platform  segmentation framework so the sort of  segmentation that we did for Tomb Raider  we're able to have those segments at the  moment those segments are kind of  extracted out and then used to build  audience lists for Facebook and so on  with the cross-platform segmentation  framework what we are intending to do is  have the segmentations native to the  system itself so that we can pass them  around as kind of first-class objects  within the architecture and finally the  all the examples I've given here have  been sort of marketing related or maybe  sales analysis related the reason for  that is  there's twofold first of all my team's  responsible for most of those websites  and stores and so on so it's within our  team's ability to do those controls we  work very closely the analytics team and  between the two of us were able to do  this without too much additional kind of  buy-in but the other thing obviously is  that the dev cycle on a marketing  campaign is weeks or months at most the  dev cycle on a game is months or years  and so it was gonna be much quicker for  us to get those kind of proof points by  working on marketing campaigns to show  the idea the kind of proof of concept of  what we want to do with user data and  segmentation but the next step for us is  then closing that loop and starting to  use that data from bigquery natively in  games to do personalization and so on  within the game itself rather than  outside the game and what's next so  that's what's next for the the sgv  platform what's next for Square Enix in  general well we are deploying game  servers on gtp for some multiplayer  titles coming up which is gonna generate  a lot of data we are also deploying a  lot of the back-end services or identity  systems billing systems store sales and  so on are being deployed in GCP as well  again it's going to generate a lot of  data excuse me  and finally we're scaling up analytics  pipeline the SUV platform itself to be  able to cope with much larger portions  of data unfortunately of those three  pillars that's the easiest for us  because that's something that pretty  much is handled entirely by Google so  the scaling side of it is something that  is no longer a big challenge for us we  just have to write slightly bigger check  that's fine and that's it I don't think  we have time for questions I've left my  email address there feel free to get in  touch if you've got any questions or  come up afterwards but otherwise thank  you very much  [Applause]
 

